                    Studio 2
                </div>
                            <div class="type-h5" aria-label="Event times">
                    3pm, 6.30pm & 7pm
                </div>
            </div>
                    </div>
                    
            </header>		
							
		
	    
					<div class="article__inner">
									<div class="article__content">
							
    <section class="main-content">
                    <div class="main-content__intro margin-trim type-body-xl">
                <p><span style="font-weight: 400;">What if your life’s work revealed secrets you didn’t even know you had…</span></p>

            </div>
        
                    <div class="main-content__text margin-trim type-body-l">
                <p><span style="font-weight: 400;">For more than thirty years, writer and filmmaker Johnny Daukes unknowingly documented a story he could neither remember nor understand. Songs, scripts and films kept returning to the same themes, each becoming another fragment of an unwitting documentary, pointing towards a truth buried deep beneath memory.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">As long-suppressed events began to surface, those creative works took on an extraordinary new significance, revealing not only Johnny’s history of childhood abuse, but the mysterious ways in which his mind had protected itself. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Following an exhausting process that came to a conclusion at the Old Bailey earlier this year, </span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">SQUID </span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">explores how art and music can preserve what memory has buried — and ultimately help bring it back into the light.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Blending live music, humour and storytelling, </span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">SQUID: A Victim Impact Statement</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;"> is a true story about memory, creativity and an accidental search for catharsis.</span></p>
<h3>Post-show talk</h3>
<p><strong>12 November: </strong>Following the performance, writer and broadcaster Peter Curran (<em>Bunk Bed</em> and <em>No Blacks No Irish</em> on BBC Radio 4) will host a brief Q&amp;A with Johnny Daukes, exploring some of the themes raised by <em>SQUID – </em>memory, abuse, creativity and the strange ways in which art can reveal things before we’re ready to understand them.</p>

            </div>
            </section>

